# Do people really smooth their spending?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "What is \"excess sensitivity\", and who lives hand to mouth? What happened to spending when stimulus cheques arrived?"
next: {"title":"How do you choose when the future is uncertain?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/84-how-do-you-choose-when-the-future-is.cast"}

## A cheque in the post
sources:
    - id: jps2006
      title: Household Expenditure and the Income Tax Rebates of 2001
      authors: D. S. Johnson, J. A. Parker, N. S. Souleles
      year: 2006
      finding: Rebates ($300 single, $600 couple) went to about two thirds of US households in a week set by the second-to-last SSN digit, July–September 2001. Households spent 20–40% of the rebate on nondurables in the 3 months of receipt, and roughly another third in the next 3 months; responses largest for low liquid wealth and low income.
      url: https://www.nber.org/papers/w10784

    box cheque "Tax rebate 2001\n$300 single\n$600 couple" cites ["jps2006"] x 290 y 470 width 400 height 230 radius 12 icon envelope font_size 36 style.stroke #b5482e hidden true
    text share "to ~⅔ of US households" #b5482e x 290 y 330 font_size 30 hidden true
    box weeks "Mailed July → Sept\nweek set by\nan SSN digit" x 720 y 470 width 400 height 230 radius 12 icon calendar font_size 34 style.stroke #2f6b8f hidden true
    box got "Cheque arrived" x 290 y 200 width 360 height 110 radius 12 icon house font_size 32 hidden true
    box wait "Still waiting" x 720 y 200 width 360 height 110 radius 12 icon hourglass font_size 32 hidden true
    scale spent cites ["jps2006"] x 100 y 430 width 800 font_size 30 label "Cents of each $1 spent in 3 months" unit ¢ min 0 max 100 value 30 ticks 10 markers [{"value":1,"label":"smoothing: <1¢","color":"#2f6b8f"}] hidden true
    scratch calc work ["$600 ÷ 40 years ≈ $15 a year","3 months ≈ $4: under 1¢ per $1"] x 620 y 250 font_size 30 hidden true
    path band cites ["jps2006"] points [[260,430],[420,430]] style.opacity 0.55 style.stroke #b5482e style.stroke_width 16 hidden true
    text found "Found: 20–40¢" #b5482e cites ["jps2006"] x 340 y 345 font_size 46 hidden true
    text who "Johnson, Parker & Souleles 2006" #8f887c x 340 y 295 font_size 26 hidden true
    text more "+ about ⅓ more in the next 3 months" #b5482e x 500 y 240 font_size 32 hidden true
    text xs "excess sensitivity" #3d3833 x 500 y 180 font_size 54 hidden true

    card title "Do people really smooth their spending?"

Do people really smooth their spending? Watch what happens when a cheque lands in the post.
    draw cheque

In the summer of 2001, the US Treasury mailed tax rebates t (@draw share@)o about two thirds of American households: three hundred dollars for a single (@highlight cheque part "$600 couple"@) person, six hundred for a couple.

B: And the week your cheque came was set by a digit of your Social Security number. Close to a lottery.
    draw weeks

Which makes it an experiment: households that already have their cheque, next to households still waiting for theirs.
    draw got wait

B: So what does smooth (@draw spent@)ing predict?
    erase cheque share weeks got wait

That a one-off six hundred dollars gets spread over th (@draw calc_box calc_line_1@)e rest of your life. Over, say, forty years, that's about fifteen dollars a year.

So in the fir (@draw calc_line_2@)st three months, less than one c (@draw spent_marker_1@)ent of each dollar.

And the rebate was law by June: a smoother adjusts when the (@erase calc@) news comes, not when the envelope does.

    ask "How many cents of each $1 went on everyday spending? Click the line." intro "Your turn." on spent store g default "60" tolerance 0.1 stage own voice b
        right: Twenty to forty cents: you said {g}, close.
        wrong: You said {g}: it was 20 to 40 cents.

Twenty to forty (@draw band found@) cents, on food, petrol and other everyday things. That's what David Johnson, Jonathan  (@draw who@)Parker and Nicholas Souleles found in 2006.

And roughly an (@draw more@)other third of the rebate in the three months after that.

    pause 0.3

Spending jumped when the money landed (@highlight spent_marker_1@), not when the news did.

Economists call that excess sen (@draw xs@)sitivity: spending that follows a change in income everyone saw coming.

    pause 0.5

## Hand to mouth
vars:
    h:
      value: 0
      min: 0
      max: 100
      step: 1
      decimals: 0
    ww:
      value: 0
      fixed: true
    m:
      value: 50
      min: 0
      max: 100
      step: 5
      decimals: 0
    avg:
      expr: h*m/100 + (100-h)/100
      decimals: 0
sources:
    - id: kvw2014
      title: The Wealthy Hand-to-Mouth
      authors: G. Kaplan, G. L. Violante, J. Weidner
      year: 2014
      finding: Brookings Papers on Economic Activity. Between 25 and 40 percent of US households are hand-to-mouth (preferred estimate one third); one third of them are poor and two thirds wealthy (little liquid wealth, sizable illiquid wealth in housing or retirement accounts; median illiquid wealth around $50,000 at age 40).
      url: https://www.nber.org/papers/w20073

    population crowd cites ["kvw2014"] fit.h 510 fit.w 580 fit.x 30 fit.y 150 colors.poor #b5482e colors.smooth #8f887c colors.wealthy #d9a521 count 100 states.smooth 100 states.poor 0 states.wealthy 0 order spread labels.poor "poor hand to mouth" labels.smooth smooth labels.wealthy "wealthy hand to mouth" bind {"states.poor":"h - ww*h*2/3","states.wealthy":"ww*h*2/3"} hidden true
