    card title "Who spends the cheque?"

Spending follows the cheque. So whose spending is it? Picture a hundred American households.
    draw crowd

B: The obvious suspects are poor families with nothing in the bank. They spend it because they have to.
    blocking false

    animate h 11 duration 2.5

Partly right. Economists call them han (@highlight crowd_poor@)d to mouth: what comes in this month goes out this month.

In 2014, Greg Kaplan, Giovan (@draw kvw@)ni Violante and Justin Weidner counted them in US survey (@animate h 33 duration 2.5@) data. About one household in three (@draw third@) lives hand to mouth.

But only a third o (@erase kvw@)f those are poor. The othe (@animate ww 1 duration 2.5@)r two thirds own a house or a pension; at age forty,  (@draw locked@)around fifty thousand dollars' worth. They call them  (@highlight crowd_wealthy@)the wealthy hand to mouth.

B: Fifty thousand (@highlight locked@) dollars of wealth, and they live paycheck to paycheck?

Because it's (@draw lock@) locked up. You can't spend a slice of your house on groceries (@highlight locked part "Cash: almost none"@); getting at it means fees, paperwork, a new loan.

And that can be a sensible choice: houses and pensions earn more than a bank acco (@highlight crowd_wealthy@)unt, so you keep little cash, and a cheque gets spent.

Now add  (@erase locked lock third@)it up, with illustrative number (@draw rd_title@)s: say a hand-to-mouth househol (@draw rd_htm@)d spends half a cheque within three months, and a smoo (@draw rd_sm@)ther about one cent per dollar.

Try it: drag the share of hand-to-mouth hous (@draw rd_avg rd_share@)eholds and how much they spend, and see what it takes to reach the twenty to forty cents that was found.

    explore params ["h","m"]

B: So a third of households with no spare cash, each spending a big slice (@highlight rd_avg@), is enough to pull the average a long way up.

    pause 0.5

## 2008, bigger
sources:
    - id: psjm2013
      title: Consumer Spending and the Economic Stimulus Payments of 2008
      authors: J. A. Parker, N. S. Souleles, D. S. Johnson, R. McClelland
      year: 2013
      finding: American Economic Review 103(6). Payments timed by the last two digits of the SSN. Households spent about 12–30% of payments on nondurables in the 3 months of receipt, and about 50–90% including durables (mainly vehicles). Larger responses for older, lower-income and home-owning households.
      url: https://www.nber.org/papers/w16684
    - id: irs2008
      title: Economic Stimulus Payments (IRS news releases, 2008)
      authors: Internal Revenue Service
      year: 2008
      finding: Up to $600 per person ($1,200 couples), minimum $300 ($600), plus $300 per child under 17; direct deposits scheduled by the last two digits of the SSN.
      url: https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-news/ir-08-066.pdf
    - id: kv2014
      title: A Model of the Consumption Response to Fiscal Stimulus Payments
      authors: G. Kaplan, G. L. Violante
      year: 2014
      finding: Econometrica 82(4). Life-cycle model with a liquid asset and a high-return illiquid asset with a transaction cost; many households end up wealthy hand-to-mouth; calibrated to the 2001 rebates it gives responses in line with the evidence (about 25% spent on nondurables in the quarter), an order of magnitude above the one-asset model.

    box pay "2008 payments\n$300–600 per adult\n+ $300 per child" cites ["irs2008"] x 330 y 520 width 420 height 210 radius 12 icon envelope font_size 34 style.stroke #b5482e hidden true
    box digits "Week set by\nlast 2 SSN digits" cites ["irs2008"] x 750 y 520 width 360 height 210 radius 12 icon calendar font_size 34 style.stroke #2f6b8f hidden true
