    path band_all cites ["psjm2013"] points [[500,330],[820,330]] style.opacity 0.45 style.stroke #3d3833 style.stroke_width 18 hidden true
    text lab_all "with cars: 50–90¢" #3d3833 cites ["psjm2013"] x 660 y 245 font_size 36 hidden true
    icon car size 70 set twemoji x 880 y 240 icon_look picture of automobile hidden true
    row who gap 40 fit.h 110 fit.w 760 fit.x 120 fit.y 520 hidden true
    box t_old "older" width 200 height 80 radius 10 font_size 34 in who hidden true
    box t_low "lower income" width 240 height 80 radius 10 font_size 34 in who hidden true
    box t_home "homeowners" width 240 height 80 radius 10 icon house font_size 34 style.stroke #d9a521 in who hidden true
    mark home_circle around t_home kind circle hidden true
    box model "Kaplan–Violante model:\nrational, patient, wealth locked up\n→ ~¼ of a rebate spent in a quarter" cites ["kv2014"] x 500 y 590 width 760 height 160 radius 12 font_size 32 style.stroke #2f6b8f hidden true

    card title "2008: the cheques again"

Locked-up wealth fits the 2001 cheques. In 2 (@draw pay@)008 the US mailed out cheques again, and bigger ones.

B: Sam (@draw digits@)e lottery?

Same trick: the week depended on t (@highlight digits@)he last two digits of your Social Security number.

(@erase pay digits@) Jonathan Parker and three co-author (@draw ruler@)s measured the response: twelve to thirty cents  (@draw band_nd lab_nd@)of each dollar on everyday spending within three months.

Count durable goods, mostly cars, and th (@draw band_all lab_all@)e total climbs to fifty to ninety cents.

B: Cars?  (@draw car@)That's not living hand to mouth, that's a down payment.

For a household with no cash to spare, a lump sum  (@highlight car@)can be exactly the down payment that was missing.

And the biggest responses came fr (@draw t_old@)om older, low (@draw t_low@)er-income and home-o (@draw t_home@)wning households.

Home (@draw home_circle@)owners: the wealthy hand to mouth again.

Kaplan and V (@erase who home_circle car@)iolante built a model of (@draw model@) rational, patient households who keep most of their wealth in houses and pensions.

It spends about a quarte (@highlight model part "→ ~¼ of a rebate spent in a quarter"@)r of a rebate in the first quarter, as the 2001 data did.

So smoothing fails where t (@highlight band_nd band_all@)he cash is missing, even in households that are rich on paper.

    pause 0.5

## Impatient, or locked in?
sources:
    - id: parker2017
      title: Why Don't Households Smooth Consumption? Evidence from a $25 Million Experiment
      authors: J. A. Parker
      year: 2017
      finding: 'AEJ: Macroeconomics 9(4). Over 25,000 Nielsen panel households receiving randomly timed 2008 payments. Low-liquidity households spend at three to four times the rate of liquid ones on arrival; the response is predicted by income two years earlier (a persistent trait, not a temporary shock) and is most strongly related to impatience, also to lack of planning; unrelated to expectation errors.'
      url: https://www.nber.org/papers/w21369
    - id: orw2025
      title: 'Micro MPCs and Macro Counterfactuals: The Case of the 2008 Rebates'
      authors: J. D. Orchard, V. A. Ramey, J. F. Wieland
      year: 2025
      finding: Quarterly Journal of Economics 140(3) (NBER WP 31584, 2023). Correcting bias in the two-way fixed-effects (staggered timing) estimates lowers the full-sample 2008 spending response from about 0.5 to about 0.3 per dollar; general-equilibrium consumption multipliers below 0.2.
      url: https://www.nber.org/papers/w31584

    box col_lock "Locked-up wealth\ncash short now\nKaplan–Violante" x 270 y 575 width 400 height 210 radius 12 icon house font_size 32 style.stroke #2f6b8f hidden true
    box col_imp "Impatience\nsame people, years before\nParker 2017" cites ["parker2017"] x 730 y 575 width 400 height 210 radius 12 icon hourglass font_size 32 style.stroke #b5482e hidden true
    scratch fact cites ["parker2017"] work ["little cash → spend 3–4× faster","predicted by income 2 years earlier"] x 500 y 320 font_size 30 hidden true
    text size "2008 total: ~50¢ or ~30¢ per $1?" cites ["orw2025"] x 500 y 430 font_size 38 hidden true
    text size_who "Orchard, Ramey & Wieland" #8f887c x 500 y 390 font_size 26 hidden true
    text st_cons "Spending jumps when the cheque lands: consensus" #2f6b8f x 500 y 430 font_size 34 hidden true
    text st_cont "Why, and how much in total: contested" #b5482e x 500 y 385 font_size 34 hidden true
    text close "With a cushion: smooth\nWithout one: spend what lands" x 500 y 420 font_size 52 hidden true

    card title "Impatient, or locked in?"

Locked- (@draw col_lock@)up wealth is one story. Jonathan Parker tested it on more than twenty-five thousand households as their 2008 payments arrived.

B: Households with little cash (@draw fact_box fact_line_1@) spent three to four times as fast as the rest, so far so good.

B: But their response cou (@draw fact_line_2@)ld be predicted from their income two years earlier.

So it isn't a bad month. It looks like (@erase fact@) a trait. And the strongest predictor  (@draw col_imp@)was impatience: people who'd rather have money now.

B: Locked-up wealth  (@highlight col_lock@)says these are planners who hold their savings in the wrong place.

B: Impatience  (@highlight col_imp@)says some people never build a cushion at all.

And the size is disputed too:  (@draw size size_who@)Jacob Orchard, Valerie Ramey and Johannes Wieland argue that, corrected for how the timing method works, the 2008 respons (@highlight size part ~30¢@)e is closer to thirty cents per dollar than fifty.

(@erase size size_who@) How sure are we? That spending j (@draw st_cons@)umps when a cheque lands is consensus.

Why it does, and how mu (@draw st_cont@)ch that adds up to for the whole economy, is contested.

    pause 0.4

    quiz "Harbourtown's council sends every household a one-off $500. Who is likely to spend most of it within weeks?" intro "Three new cases."
        right: The welder: wealthy on paper, but his wealth is in the house, and the $500 is the only spare cash he has.
        wrong: Look for wealth that can't be spent this month: here, the welder with the house and the empty account.
        * A retired teacher with $40,000 in the bank
        + A boatyard welder with a mortgaged house and $200 in his account
        * A young renter with $10,000 saved

    quiz "Harbourtown's school-bus drivers are paid only from September to June, and everyone knows it. What does a perfect smoother's spending do in July?" voice b
        right: It stays about the same: she saves from the paid months to cover the summer she saw coming.
        wrong: A smoother plans for a gap she knows is coming: here, it stays about the same.
        * It falls sharply
        + It stays about the same
        * It rises

    quiz "A government wants a stimulus that is spent within months. Which design gets the most spent?"
        right: Cheques to households with little cash: they have no cushion to put the money in, so it gets spent.
        wrong: Who has no cushion to park the money in? Here, households with little cash.
        * The same cheque for everyone
        + Cheques to households with little cash
        * A tax cut for top earners

Do people (@erase col_lock col_imp st_cons st_cont@) smooth? Those with a cushion of cash (@draw close@) mostly do. The other third spend what lands,

and the next question is what that cushi (@highlight close part "With a cushion: smooth"@)on is for when the future is uncertain.

    pause 0.5

## Where next
